# Cultural Landscape Report for Gettysburg National Military Park: Record of Treatment, Volume II (Layton et al., OCLP 2018): Areas 11-15
# (Houck's Ridge-Rose-Snyder-Warfield, Big Round Top-Devil's Den-Plank-Weikert, Little Round Top, South Cavalry Field-Hammer/Bushman-Slyder, East Cavalry Field).
# Quotes typed in the corrected spelling ('=' form, fi ligatures lost in the text layer). Documentation bases are the report's own statements.
@ NPS-CLR-ROT-V2
%basis the report's endnotes: damage claims, the 1863 Bachelder, Cope and Elliott maps, the 1868 Warren map, the 1872 survey, the 1880 Bachelder/Maxson map, photographs (Tipton, Boardman, park collections)
93 | fields | Rose and Snyder fields: open, avenue of approach; stone fence and boulders along the Union line | bld-rose-farm,bld-snyder-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Rose and Snyder fields were open and an avenue of approach and observation for Confederate forces, with a Union line posted along and behind a stone fence and rock; the report says the open character is documented in the 1863 Bachelder and 1872 maps, a 1900 survey, a 1936 aerial photograph and photographs | =The Rose and Snyder fields provided an avenue of approach and observation for Confederate forces. The open character of the fields is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map, 1872 topographical survey map, 1900 Gettysburg National Park Commission Survey, a 1936 aerial photograph, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection and the park’s historical collection
97 | orchards | Rose North Orchard documented in the 1863 Bachelder map | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Rose North Orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder map, the 1872 survey, a 1900 survey and photographs; the park completed its replanting in 2006 | =The orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map, 1872 topographical survey map, 1900 Gettysburg National Park Commission Survey, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection and the park’s historical collection (Figure 27).12 In 2006, the park completed replanting of the Rose North Orchard.
98 | orchards | Rose orchard burials: Semmes's Brigade 'Jno Roses Place orchard, near fence' | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | A local physician's list of Confederate interments made the year after the battle places Semmes's Brigade soldiers at 'Jno Roses Place orchard, near fence', meaning the orchard near the springhouse on the Rose Run South Headwaters | =According to entries in the list of Confederate interments made by a local physician in the year following the battle, soldiers from Semmes’s Brigade were buried at “Jno Roses Place orchard, near fence,” meaning the orchard near the springhouse on the Rose Run South Headwaters.
98 | orchards | Rose Farm Orchard east of Rose Field 8 documented in the 1872 survey | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Rose Farm Orchard, east of Rose Field 8, likely served as an avenue of approach and an obstacle to the eastward advance and is documented in the 1872 survey and photographs | =The Rose Farm Orchard, located east of Rose Field 8, likely served as an avenue of approach and created an obstacle to the eastward advance.17 The orchard is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map and in photographs from the Tipton Collection and the park’s historical collection
99 | orchards | Rose Old Orchard documented in photographs only | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Rose Old Orchard, east of Rose Field 8, is documented in photographs and the park's collection, not in an 1863 map | =The Rose Old Orchard, located east of Rose Field 8, likely served as an avenue of approach and created an obstacle to the eastward advance.18 The orchard is documented in photographs from William A. Frassanito’s Early Photography at Gettysburg and the park’s historical collection
100 | orchards | Snyder orchard documented in the 1863 Bachelder, Cope and Elliott maps | bld-snyder-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Snyder orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder, Cope and Elliott maps, the 1872 survey and park photographs; the farmstead was in the Confederate line on Seminary Ridge July 2-4; the park replanted the orchard in 2007 | =Consequently, Union counter fire damaged the farmstead and Confederates used the buildings as emergency shelter.20 The orchard likely provided cover for Confederate troops and is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map, 1863 Cope Map, 1863 Elliott Map, 1872 topographical survey map, and in photographs from the park’s historical collection (Figure 37).21 In 2007, the park completed replanting of the Snyder Orchard.
101 | fences | Fence on the south side of the Sherfy Peach Orchard (2nd New Hampshire) | bld-sherfy-farm,FEN-sherfy-orchard | 1863-07-02 | stated | Col. Bailey moved the 2nd New Hampshire to the left 'behind the fence that bounded the southern side of the Peach Orchard', where the fence gave cover and an obstacle to Confederate movement | =During the afternoon of July 2, Colonel E. L. Bailey moved the 2nd New Hampshire regiment of Burling’s Brigade to the left, “behind the fence that bounded the southern side of the Peach Orchard, where it might be partly concealed and covered.”22 This fencing type provided cover for Union troops and additionally created an obstacle to Confederate movements.
101 | fences | Worm fencing along the Sherfy Peach Orchard and Rose North Orchard documented in the 1863 Bachelder map | bld-sherfy-farm,FEN-sherfy-orchard | 1863-07 | stated | The worm fencing along the boundaries of the Sherfy Peach Orchard and Rose North Orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder map, the 1872 survey and Tipton photographs | =The worm fencing along the boundaries of the Sherfy Peach Orchard and Rose North Orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map, 1872 topographical survey map, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection
102 | fences | Rose West Woods north boundary worm fence along Wheatfield Road | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The fencing paralleling the south edge of Wheatfield Road was an obstacle to Union forces and was removed; worm fencing on the north boundary of Rose West Woods is documented in the 1872 survey and Tipton and Boardman photographs | =The fencing, paralleling the south edge of Wheatfield Road, proved to be an obstacle to Union forces and was removed.26 Worm fencing along the north boundary of Rose West Woods is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map and in photographs from the Tipton Collection and the Boardman Collection
103 | fences | Fences on the north and west of the Rose Wheatfield | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The fence lines along the north and west of the Rose Wheatfield are documented as a wooden fence in the 1863 Bachelder map and as worm fence in the 1868 Warren map, the 1872 survey and photographs; the fence gave cover to and obstructed both forces | =This fencing type provided cover to and created an obstacle for both forces. Fence lines along the north and west perimeters of the Rose Wheatfield are documented as a wooden fence in the 1863 Bachelder Map and as worm fence in the 1868 Warren Map, 1872 topographical survey map, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection, the Boardman Collection, and the park’s historical collection
103 | fences | Two fences lined the Emmitsburg Road; worm fence east boundary of Rose Fields 1-3 and Snyder Field 1 | FEN-emmitsburg-road,bld-rose-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | Col. Aiken of the 7th South Carolina recalled 'sharpshooters injured us while climbing the two fences that lined the pike (Emmitsburg Road)'; the report says the worm fencing on the east boundary of Rose Fields 1-3 and Snyder Field 1 is documented in the 1868 Warren map | =Having advanced across Rose Field 2, the colonel recalled that, “Sharpshooters injured us while climbing the two fences that lined the pike [Emmitsburg Road].”28 Worm fencing along the east boundary of Rose Fields 1–3 and Snyder Field 1 created an obstacle to Confederate troop movements and is documented in the 1868 Warren Map.
104 | fences | Worm fence on the west boundary of Rose Field 8 | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The worm fencing on the west boundary of Rose Field 8 is documented as a wooden fence in the 1863 Bachelder map and as a worm fence in the 1868 Warren map and 1872 survey | =Worm fencing along the west boundary of Rose Field 8 created an obstacle to Confederate forces advancing east and is documented as a wooden fence in the 1863 Bachelder Map and as a worm fence in the 1868 Warren Map and 1872 topographical survey map
104 | fences | Longstreet's instruction to 'grab the bottom rail and turn it over': fences on Emmitsburg Road or Snyder field boundaries | FEN-emmitsburg-road,bld-snyder-farm | 1863-07-02 | hedged | Based on the movements of the 3rd Arkansas, the report says Longstreet's instruction to turn over the bottom rail may refer to fences along Emmitsburg Road or the west boundaries of Snyder Fields 3 and 7, which it says are documented as wooden fence in 1863 and worm fence in 1868 and 1872 | =Based on the movements of the 3rd Arkansas, Longstreet’s instructions may have referred to fences along Emmitsburg Road or the west boundaries of Snyder Fields 3 and 7. The worm fencing in both locations created an obstacle to Confederate forces and is documented as a wooden fence in the 1863 Bachelder Map and as a worm fence in the 1868 Warren Map
105 | fences | Picket fence around the Rose yard and garden | bld-rose-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The picket fencing at the Rose yard and garden obstructed Confederate forces and is documented in Tipton photographs; the park built 533 feet of picket fence and a gate there in 2012 | =The picket fencing at the Rose Yard and Garden created an obstacle to Confederate forces and is documented in photographs from the Tipton Collection (Figure 53).32 In 2012, the park completed the installation of 533 feet of picket fence and one 3-foot wide gate along the perimeter of the Rose Yard and Garden
140 | fields | Devil's Den open character documented in the 1863 Bachelder and Cope maps | - | 1863-07 | stated | The open character of Devil's Den is documented in the 1863 Bachelder and Cope maps and in Tipton, Boardman, Frassanito and park photographs | =The open character of Devil’s Den is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map, 1863 Cope Map, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection, the Boardman Collection, William A. Frassanito’s Early Photography at Gettysburg, and the park’s historical collection
141 | woodlots | Sherfy Thicket and Plum Run Gorge: immature thicket | bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The vegetation of the Sherfy Thicket was immature enough that soldiers in it were exposed to small arms and artillery fire; the thicket and Plum Run Gorge are documented in the 1863 Bachelder map and park photographs | =Sherfy Thicket served as an avenue of approach for both forces with the thicket vegetation immature enough that soldiers within it were exposed to small arms and artillery fire. The character of the Sherfy Thicket and Plum Run Gorge is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map and in photographs from the park’s historical collection
143 | fences | Houck field west boundary stone and rider fence, documented in the 1863 Bachelder and Cope maps | P-05 | 1863-07 | stated | A stone fence at the western edge of Houck Field gave cover to both forces and its stone components are documented in the 1863 Bachelder and Cope maps | =This fencing type provided cover and created an obstacle for both forces at the western edge of Houck Field and its stone components are documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map and 1863 Cope Map.
169 | woodlots | Douglass Woods documented in the 1863 Cope map | - | 1863-07 | stated | Douglass Woods, a long line of woods on the west side of the Emmitsburg Road, gave Confederate troops cover on the second and third days and is documented in the 1863 Cope map, the 1872 survey and photographs | =The woodlot is documented in the 1863 Cope Map, 1872 topographical survey map, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection and the park’s historical collection
170 | orchards | Bushman/Hammer orchard inferred from a claim for bees | bld-bushman-farm | 1863-07 | hedged | The Bushman/Hammer application for damages names no orchard but claims five skeps of bees, which the report takes to support the conclusion that the farm kept an orchard documented in the 1872 survey and photographs | =No specific reference was made to an orchard in the Bushman/Hammer application for damage compensation; however, a claim was made for five scaps of bees with patent boxes, costing $7 a piece, which had been broken and the honey taken.13 This investment in bees supports the conclusion that the farm maintained an orchard and the feature is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map and in historic photographs from the Tipton Collection and the park’s historical collection
189 | orchards | Lott orchard documented in the 1880 Bachelder/Maxson map | - | 1863-07 (documented by an 1880 map) | stated | The Lott orchard on East Cavalry Field is documented in the 1880 Bachelder/Maxson map and in Tipton photographs, and the Union 1st Brigade of the Cavalry Corps' 2nd Division probably used it for cover | =The Union 1st Brigade, 2nd Division Cavalry Corps, under command of Colonel John B. McIntosh, likely used the Lott Orchard for cover. The orchard is documented in the 1880 Bachelder/Maxson Map and in photographs from the Tipton Collection
